# Reviewed RoadWeaver interop artifact SHA-256 allowlist.
# One lowercase hex SHA-256 per line. Lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# The GreatWall RoadWeaverAdapter refuses to initialize the soft-dependency
# bridge unless the RoadWeaver jar loaded at runtime matches an entry here.
# Regenerate this file after rebuilding/upgrading RoadWeaver and re-reviewing
# its interop ABI (PlanningInteropRegistry / WallInteropTypes / RoadNetworkApi
# registerStructureEndpointOnce signature).
#
# Exactly one digest is admitted, and RoadWeaverAllowlistTest pins that: a jar
# whose SHA is absent makes RoadWeaverAdapter refuse to initialise, and
# docs/release/compatibility.json must name this same digest.
#
# 2026-10-08: moved from 0.8.406-perf-batch (bed13208...) back to
# 0.8.405-audit-io-degradation-and-counter-cleanup, rebuilt from RoadWeaver
# commit eaf9892, which reverts 821511c. ABI re-reviewed before switching, as
# the header above requires: the four interop types this adapter touches are
# byte-identical between the two builds - EndpointHeightAgreementApi 1056 B,
# PlanningInteropRegistry 12395 B, WallInteropTypes 1168 B, RoadNetworkApi
# 20604 B in both. 821511c's diff is confined to pathfinding and storage
# internals (RoadGradePlanner, StructureAvoidanceService,
# RoadGenerationService, the four pathfinders, CoarseTerrainRegion,
# StructureFileStorage, CoarseHeightAwarePlanner) and touches no interop type,
# so the reviewed contract still holds.
#
# 2026-10-09: 0.8.405-audit-io-degradation-and-counter-cleanup ->
# 0.8.407-wall-prewarm-gate. The change is additive to startup ordering only:
# InitialGenManager calls a new WallPrewarmBridge before any road work, and the
# wall's prewarm is now signalled by the river patch the moment the rivers are
# built, so the wall no longer runs alongside road generation. ABI re-reviewed
# before switching, as the header requires, and all four interop types this
# adapter touches are again byte-identical - EndpointHeightAgreementApi 1056 B,
# PlanningInteropRegistry 12395 B, WallInteropTypes 1168 B, RoadNetworkApi
# 20604 B in both builds. The new bridge lives in
# net.shiroha233.roadweaver.generation and reaches the wall by reflection, so it
# adds no type this adapter depends on.
#
# 2026-10-09 (rebuilt): same version, second build. The gate moved from
# blockUntilDone to the top of begin(), and the wait now uses
# MinecraftServer.managedBlock so the server thread keeps draining its queue
# while it waits - without that the wall, whose prewarm completes through that
# same queue, could never finish and the two would wait on each other. Only
# InitialGenManager and WallPrewarmBridge changed; the four interop types above
# were re-checked and are unchanged again.
#
# 2026-10-09 (rebuilt again): the managedBlock wait deadlocked - it runs queued
# server tasks but not the chunk generation the wall's root search needs. The
# gate is now non-blocking: begin() returns immediately with a deferred flag when
# the wall is not done, and a server-tick hook re-enters once it is. Only
# InitialGenManager, WallPrewarmBridge and the Fabric-side mixin/hook changed.
# The four interop types were re-checked a third time and are unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-09 (rebuilt once more): the deadlock was not the managedBlock wait but a
# broken recursive driver in the wall's root search, now fixed. The gate is
# blocking again, as the user requires - the player stays on the loading screen
# until the wall finishes - via managedBlock so the server still drains its own
# queue. The four interop types are re-checked a fourth time and are unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-09 (fifth build): the real deadlock was two event loops on the server
# thread. The wall posted addTicket/request to the world-local chunk executor
# (ServerChunkCache$MainThreadExecutor), while the road side's managedBlock drains
# only the global MinecraftServer queue and skips the chunk queues before the tick
# loop starts (haveTime() false), so the wall starved waiting on its own chunk
# request. The wall now posts to level.getServer() (the global queue), and the
# road side's wait explicitly pumps server.pollTask() plus every level's
# chunkSource().pollTask(). The four interop types are re-checked a fifth time and
# are unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-09 (sixth build, plan B): blocking the server thread at all is wrong. The
# road side no longer blocks on the wall; the prepareLevels mixin only marks a gate
# and returns, leaving the server thread free to tick so the wall's structure screen
# can run chunk requests, and a server-tick hook runs the real begin once the wall
# reports done. The wall's chunk work reverts to its original world-local executor.
# The four interop types are re-checked a sixth time and are unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-09 (seventh build): the non-blocking gate let the player enter before the
# wall finished, because prepareLevels returned early. Restored the blocking wait, but
# it pumps BOTH event loops - the global server queue and every level's chunk queue -
# so the wall's chunk requests (which must run on the server thread, and are posted to
# the world-local chunk executor) keep making progress while the road side blocks. The
# wall's chunk work stays on its world-local executor. The four interop types are
# re-checked a seventh time and are unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-09 (ninth build): the eighth build's road-side reflective warming() call
# crashed - it ran warming() a second time, and the wall's prepareFrozen() rejects
# "inputs already frozen". The mixin-order fix is correct and sufficient on its own:
# the wall's probe mixin is pinned to priority 950 (before the roads' 1000), so the
# road side needs no defensive warming() call. Reverted to the seventh build's bridge
# (same SHA). The four interop types are re-reviewed and remain byte-identical.
#
# 2026-10-10 (tenth build): audit fixes. WallPrewarmBridge no longer blocks on the
# ForkJoinPool.commonPool (a named daemon waiter thread instead), and the wall side
# resets RIVERS_READY/PrewarmProgress across sessions, retries a refused structure-vote
# lane submit, and hardens FeaturesLease against close-during-load. Only the bridge
# changed on the road side; the four interop types are re-reviewed again and unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-11 (eleventh build): audit follow-ups. ConfigService.load now publishes a
# fully migrated+sanitized instance (no concurrent half-updated read), and the initial
# planning wait is bounded so a stalled planning future aborts instead of hanging the
# loading screen forever. The four interop types are re-reviewed and unchanged again.
#
# 2026-10-11 (twelfth build): the wall owns the main thread for its own stage. The wall
# now pumps both event loops (managedBlock to skip shouldRun + a world-local chunk-queue
# drain) until its prewarm settles, so the roads enter and pass the gate in zero ms. The
# road side keeps the same dual pump as a defensive fallback. The four interop types are
# re-reviewed and unchanged.
#
# 2026-10-11 (fourteenth build): allow null interop in planning. validateExternalRequest no
# longer rejects a null request when a wall provider is registered - a null request is the
# deliberate wall-agnostic planning path (pure terrain; the wall is consulted only at
# construction time via the protection-gap dead end). Only RoadTerrainPlanningPipeline and
# its test changed; the four interop types are re-reviewed and unchanged.
a9954be8d6610052847778fdca8cfbece33d3212b9fe1c03d91214e56736b0a4
